{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "import math\ndef next_Perfect_Square(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the smallest perfect square strictly greater than n.\n    Example:\n        next_Perfect_Square(35) == 36\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an integer\")\n    if n < 0:\n        return 0\n    root = math.isqrt(n)\n    if root * root > n:\n        return root * root\n    return (root + 1) * (root + 1)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    # dp_prev[v] stores the number of sequences of length (current_length - 1) ending with value v.\n    # We use 1-based indexing for sequence values, so the array size is m + 1.\n    dp_prev = [0] * (m + 1)\n\n    # Base case: for length 1, each integer from 1 to m is a valid sequence.\n    for v in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp_prev[v] = 1\n\n    # Iterate for sequence lengths from 2 up to n.\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        # Calculate prefix sums of dp_prev to efficiently find the sum of previous counts.\n        # prefix_sums[i] holds the sum of dp_prev values from index 1 to i.\n        prefix_sums = [0] * (m + 1)\n        current_sum = 0\n        for i in range(1, m + 1):\n            current_sum += dp_prev[i]\n            prefix_sums[i] = current_sum\n\n        # dp_cur will store the counts for the current sequence length.\n        dp_cur = [0] * (m + 1)\n        # For each possible ending value 'v' (from 1 to m) for the current length.\n        for v in range(1, m + 1):\n            # The previous element 'u' must satisfy u <= v / 2.\n            # So, the maximum possible value for the previous element is floor(v / 2).\n            max_prev_value = v // 2\n            if max_prev_value >= 1:\n                # The number of sequences ending with 'v' is the sum of sequences\n                # of the previous length that ended with any value 'u' from 1 to max_prev_value.\n                dp_cur[v] = prefix_sums[max_prev_value]\n\n        # The dp_cur array becomes dp_prev for the next iteration (rolling array optimization).\n        dp_prev = dp_cur\n\n    # After iterating through all lengths up to n, dp_prev contains the counts for sequences of length n.\n    # The total number of sequences is the sum of all counts from dp_prev[1] to dp_prev[m].\n    return sum(dp_prev[1:])"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(array: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array.\n    A bitonic subsequence is first strictly increasing and then strictly decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(array)\n\n    # msis[i] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at array[i].\n    msis = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        msis[i] = array[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if array[j] < array[i] and msis[j] + array[i] > msis[i]:\n                msis[i] = msis[j] + array[i]\n\n    # msds[i] stores the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at array[i].\n    # This is computed by iterating from right to left.\n    msds = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        msds[i] = array[i]\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if array[j] < array[i] and msds[j] + array[i] > msds[i]:\n                msds[i] = msds[j] + array[i]\n\n    # The maximum sum bitonic subsequence is msis[i] + msds[i] - array[i].\n    # array[i] is subtracted to avoid double-counting the peak element.\n    max_sum_bitonic = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        current_bitonic_sum = msis[i] + msds[i] - array[i]\n        if current_bitonic_sum > max_sum_bitonic:\n            max_sum_bitonic = current_bitonic_sum\n\n    return max_sum_bitonic"}
